very schoolchild learns about the mutually beneficial dance of honeybees and flowers: The bee collects nectar and pollen to make honey and, in the process, spreads the flowers’ genes far and wide. In The Botany of Desire, Michael Pollan ingeniously demonstrates how people and domesticated plants have formed a\r\nsimilarly reciprocal relationship. He masterfully links four fundamental human desires—sweetness, beauty, intoxication, and control—with the plants that satisfy them: the apple, the tulip, marijuana, and the potato. In telling the stories of four familiar species, Pollan illustrates how the plants have evolved to satisfy humankind’s most basic yearnings. And just as we’ve benefited from these plants, we have also done well by them. So who is really domesticating whom?"},"oclc_numbers":["45375721"],"latest_revision":22,"revision":22,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2009-05-29T09:41:05.719594"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2024-12-27T04:15:18.247276"}},{"publishers":["Thorndike Press"],"identifiers":{"goodreads":["527320"]},"subtitle":"A Plants-Eye View of the World","weight":"1.4 pounds","covers":[4974859],"physical_format":"Hardcover","key":"/books/OL9426305M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL539266A"}],"subjects":["Science/Mathematics","Plants - General","Human-plant relationships","Science","Gardening / Horticulture","Nature","Ecology","History","Life Sciences - Botany","Large type books"],"edition_name":"Largeprint edition","languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"first_sentence":{"type":"/type/text","value":"If you happened to find yourself on the banks of the Ohio River on a particular afternoon in the springs of 1806-somewhere just to the north of Wheeling, West Virginia, say-you would probably have noticed a strange makeshift craft drifting lazily down the river."},"source_records":["amazon:0783896417:2-ag:1829197396:276440","marc:marc_loc_updates/v36.i35.records.utf8:3188395:761","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part28.ut